 -- wentywenty <2321901849@qq.com>  Tue, 13 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0800

robopi-analyze (1.0.9-1) stable; urgency=medium

  * Ship analyze-can-asc (ASC periodicity/jitter/gap analysis) as a packaged
    command depending on python3-can; it previously lived only as an unpackaged
    copy in /usr/local/bin. It now also accepts a session directory instead
    of the can.asc path.
  * analyze-can-asc: compute jitter robustly — intervals beyond 2x the median
    period are excluded from the stddev and counted separately, so a single
    long stop no longer drowns the real sub-millisecond jitter (a 160 s stop
    previously inflated the stddev to ~542 ms via a single sample).
  * analyze-can-asc: attribute oversized gaps three ways instead of calling
    them all "lost frames" — channel-wide silence of a second or more is a
    bus-idle event (transmitter stopped), shorter channel-synchronized halts
    are summarized as scheduler-beat stalls, and only gaps that occur while
    other ids keep transmitting are flagged as suspected loss.
  * analyze-can-asc: exclude event-like ids (average rate below 1 Hz, e.g.
    Device_Cmd 0x7FF config bursts) from periodicity tables, and widen table
    columns to the widest cell so large values no longer glue columns.

 -- wentywenty <2321901849@qq.com>  Thu, 18 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0800

robopi-analyze (1.0.8-1) stable; urgency=medium

  * Rebase each flushed can.asc chunk onto the session's first-frame
    timestamp before appending: log2asc normalizes every input file to
    start at 0, so the incrementally assembled can.asc restarted its
    timeline on every flush interval, making mid-capture jitter and gap
    analysis report bogus values (negative intervals, inflated stddev).
  * Raise TimeoutStopSec from 30s to 300s: teardown's full log2asc
    conversion on a long high-traffic session exceeded 30s and was killed
    before manifest.json got its ended_at_unix.

 -- wentywenty <2321901849@qq.com>  Thu, 18 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0800

robopi-analyze (1.0.7-1) stable; urgency=medium

  * Auto-rotate a running capture into a fresh session every
    SESSION_MAX_DURATION_SECS (default 1800), so no single session's files
    grow unbounded over a long unattended run. Rotation reuses the exact
    same teardown as a real stop (can.asc conversion, pcap copy, manifest
    ended_at_unix), then starts a new session; an external stop signal
    always takes priority over a pending rotation.
  * Replace the session-count-based cleanup rule with a total-size budget
    (SESSION_MAX_TOTAL_MB, default 8000): "keep the last N sessions" is
    not a meaningful proxy for disk usage once sessions can auto-rotate on
    a timer, since N sessions might be 5 hours or 5 days of data depending
    on how fast they are produced. The newest session per root is still
    always protected regardless of size.

 -- wentywenty <2321901849@qq.com>  Wed, 17 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0800

robopi-analyze (1.0.6-1) stable; urgency=high

  * Fix data corruption in the periodic ring pcap flush: on wraparound the
    ring file is truncated and rewritten by tcpdump, but the flush loop
    kept appending onto the session's existing copy instead of replacing
    it, producing an invalid multi-segment pcap file that could grow far
    past the 64 MB ring cap. Ring files are bounded and always fully
    rewritten on change, so the flush now mirrors the current content on
    every mtime change instead of tracking a byte offset.

